{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And He called to Moses.</b> Before every utterance and every saying and every command came a call — a term of endearment, a term that the ministering angels use, as it says ״וְקָרָא זֶה אֶל זֶה״ (Isaiah 6). But to the prophets of the nations of the world He revealed Himself in a term of happenstance and impurity, as it says ״וַיִּקָּר אֱלֹהִים אֶל בִּלְעָם״ (Numbers 23)." "<b>And He called to Moses.</b> The voice would go and reach his ears and all Israel did not hear it. One might say the call was also for the pauses — so it says ״וַיְדַבֵּר״ — the call was for speech and not for the pauses. And what did the pauses serve? To give Moses breathing space to reflect between one parashah and another and between one subject and another — all the more so for an ordinary person learning from an ordinary person." "<b>To him.</b> To exclude Aaron. Rabbi Yehudah says: Thirteen utterances were said in the Torah to Moses and Aaron, and corresponding to them thirteen exclusions were stated, to teach you that they were not said to Aaron but to Moses, that he should say them to Aaron. And these are the thirteen exclusions: לְדַבֵּר אִתּוֹ, מִדַּבֵּר אֵלָיו, וַיְדַבֵּר אֵלָיו (Numbers 7), וְנוֹעַדְתִּי לְךָ (Exodus 25) — all of them in Torat Kohanim. One might say they heard the sound of the utterance — so it says ״קוֹל לוֹ״, ״קוֹל אֵלָיו״ — Moses heard and all Israel did not hear." "<b>From the Tent of Meeting.</b> This teaches that the sound was cut off and did not go outside the tent. One might say because the sound was low — so it says ״אֶת הַקּוֹל״: what is ״the sound״? It is the sound spelled out in Psalms: ״קוֹל ה׳ בַּכֹּחַ קוֹל ה׳ בֶּהָדָר קוֹל ה׳ שֹׁבֵר אֲרָזִים״ (Psalms 29). If so, why does it say ״מֵאֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״? To teach that the sound was cut off. Like its parallel: ״וְקוֹל כַּנְפֵי הַכְּרוּבִים נִשְׁמַע עַד הֶחָצֵר הַחִיצֹנָה״ (Ezekiel 10) — one might say the sound was low, so it says ״כְּקוֹל אֵל שַׁדַּי בְּדַבְּרוֹ״. If so, why does it say ״עַד הֶחָצֵר הַחִיצֹנָה״? Because once it reached there it was cut off." "<b>From the Tent of Meeting, saying.</b> One might say from the entire house — so it says ״מֵעַל הַכַּפֹּרֶת״. One might say from the entire cover — so it says ״מִבֵּין שְׁנֵי הַכְּרֻבִים״." "<b>Saying.</b> Go and tell them words of endearment: 'It was with you that He spoke with me.' For we find that all forty years that Israel were in the wilderness, as if banished — from the spies onward — the divine speech was not addressed exclusively to Moses, as it says: ״וַיְהִי כַאֲשֶׁר תַּמּוּ כָּל אַנְשֵׁי הַמִּלְחָמָה לָמוּת … וַיְדַבֵּר ה' אֵלַי לֵאמֹר״ (Deuteronomy 2) — 'to me' was the speech. Another interpretation: Go and tell them My words and report back to Me whether they accept them, as it says: ״וַיָּשֶׁב מֹשֶׁה אֶת דִּבְרֵי הָעָם״ וְגוֹ' (Exodus 19)."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {2 "Rashi lists only four of the thirteen exclusions and refers to the rest as being found in Torat Kohanim."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:04:49.710256771Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}